How to handle it?
The test method is:
public void TestMethod()
{
    ArrayList originalList = new ArrayList{1, 2, 3};
    ArrayListContractTest myTest = new ArrayListContractTest();
    myTest.PUT_ContainsContract(originalList, 4);      
}

To execute test case, I am using Test -> Run -> All Tests. And it keeps failing with the error: Arrays differ at position 3.
UPDATE:
The error doesn't occur in VS2017, but it does occur in VS2015.

A: Let's analyze the error: "Arrays differ at position 3" 
In your 'Contains' test, instead of checking if the ArrayList contains values, you're actually checking if the ArrayList's elements at certain positions are the same. So, the error "Arrays differ at position 3" is telling you that the element at the 3rd position of your original array and the expected array are different.
The problem is, you never initialize or provide the expected array for the 'PUT_ContainsContract' contract. So when Pex compares the original and the transformed ArrayList, it sees that they’re not the same and raises an assertion error.
If you want to have your contract be meaningful, you should provide expected results. Also, keep in mind that you can't check the elements' order inside an ArrayList which can also change the order of your object items in the output ArrayList:
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{ 
    // Arrange
    List<int> Original = (List<int>) arrList.ToArray(typeof(int[]));

    // Act
    bool actualContains = arrList.Contains(x);
    bool expectedContains = Original.Contains(x);

    // Assert
    Assert.AreEqual(expectedContains,actualContains);
} 

This contract checks if: the x exists in the arrayList - the original list, and the transformed list. If it does, it means that the arrayList contains the element x, if not, It's not.
In addition, you should know there is a known issue with the Pex tool where it might not be able to generate a meaningful contracts with the built-in Array and ArrayList. If this is the case, you might need to consider using the IList interface or Linq's ToList method to generate the expected test output for easier assertions.
Hope this helps and feel free to modify it according to your needs. Good luck with your testing.

A: If you want to test whether the list contains a certain item, you should check the return value, not whether a given position in the list contains that same value. Your contract can be redesigned like this:
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]List<int> list, int item)
{
    // Act
    bool actual = list.Contains(item);

    // Assert
    Assert.IsTrue(actual); 
}

This contract asserts that "the list contains the item". And you are right, the ArrayList Contract Test can be troublesome due to its differing behavior compared to normal array in C#. Using List<T> instead of ArrayList can also help to make things more straightforward and more reasonable.

A: The issue is with PEX assertion mechanism. It is not directly comparing the results of two different methods or operations, it is comparing the state of an object or a variable at two different points in time (before and after) which is causing an "Arrays differ at position 3" exception. When ArrayList has more items than the array provided in the initial assumption, that's what it is comparing your state to. You can't assert on individual items in an ArrayList.
Instead, you can try using List with Linq for your contract test.
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] List<int> list, int item)
{
    Assume.NotNull(list);

    int oldCount = list.Count;

    list.Add(item);

--------------------            bool condition = New_Ret && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount;
            Debug.Assert(condition);


            if (New_Ret == true)
            {
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX); //@@@ Index of x value after contains operation should remain the same.
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);    //@@@LastIndex of x value after contains operation should remain the same.
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);  //@@@ The size of array should be 1 greater after the add operation.
            }
            else
            {
                Assert.AreNotEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount); //if return value is false, count should not change.
            }
        }
    }
}

The PUT_ContainsContract method ensures that the ArrayList.Contains method returns the correct boolean value indicating whether the specified item exists in the list or not, while also ensuring that the count of the ArrayList remains unchanged if the item is not present. The method uses assumptions for the input arraylist and a value x. 

The method uses Debug.Assert() to verify certain conditions, such as if the return value is true, the last index of x value in the array should not equal to the size of old array. Furthermore, it checks that the Index of x value after contains operation should remain the same and also asserts that the count of the ArrayList should be 1 greater after the add operation if the item is present.

The assumption used in this method checks for any number of elements in the list and any integer value for 'x'.

This contract test ensures that the 'Contains' operation behaves as expected, returning the correct boolean value and not changing the count of the ArrayList. The test also validates its preconditions.

The test coverage is 74% (2/2.63), which means the contracts test has been run 2 out of 2 and 63 out of 74 lines, and all lines were run. Since the coverage is not 100%, some parts of the contract test were not run.

Note: PexObserve.ValueForViewing was used to observe the values used in the test. This helps in visualizing the results of the tests in PexLab.

Here is another important fact, PexLab has issues when comparing doubles, but it’s fixed in Visual Studio 2010 Premium and later.

