            if (New_Ret)
            {
		Debug.Assert( New_arrListLastIndexOfX != Old_arrListCount);
	    // An Assertion for Old_arrList
            // If the return value is true, then the last position of x in old array is same as new array index of x (and last index).
            if (Old_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX)
            {	
		Debug.Assert( Old_arrListIndexOfX ==  New_arrListIndexOfX);
                }
            }



            PexAssume.True( (Old_arrListCount == 0 && New_arrListCount == 0) || (Old_arrListCount > 0 && New_arrListCount >= Old_arrListCount));

        }
    }
}

Please help me to understand a situation where I can use these contracts. 


*

*Where I can use these contracts on public methods under 'AssumePrecondition'?

*Am I implementing these contracts rightly on given example?

*What to do when I find the contracts inadequate?


Please guide me in solving this problem.

A: I can provide three clarifications to your questions.


*

*AssumePrecondition can be applied to any method in your test, but they're typically used for methods that have a significant precondition that is safe to assume will hold true for all inputs. In your example, you're correctly using it before PUT_ContainsContract method.

*Your use of PexObserve is a good practice, it helps you in understanding how your method is working. PexObserve.ValueForViewing allows you to see the values of different properties as the program runs, which is always helpful in debugging.

*When you find a contract inadequate, the next step is quite straightforward: you need to find out where exactly the contract failed. You can use PexObserve to record current values and check these records by using PexExplorer.


In addition, I believe your ArrayListContractTest is incomplete. Even after you've written everything, you should try to verify your tests by using the settings in your PexRunSettings file. In particular, you might want to turn on generation of code coverage targets to ensure that your test coverage is reasonable and complete.

As to PEX/Contracts, they help during state exploration. They are there to test your contract preconditions and the postconditions of your methods. It is important to maintain consistent values as part of a method's behavior.

Also, always ensure that your method under test changes its state based solely on the assumption made in AssumePrecondition. Otherwise, PEX may give incorrect results.

The best way to understand contract testing on your own would be to go through the PEX samples provided by Microsoft in their official documentation. They have a range of examples covering various aspects of Contracts usage.

A: The PexContract tests that you've written are correct and are used for testing contracts in your code. When you create such tests, Pex checks if those Contracts hold true in your code.


*

*You can use the AssumePrecondition attribute on any method where the method's expected behavior depends on the initial state of the test. This allows Pex to consider the behavior of the method with different initial state. One example might be checking the Add method in a list which checks that the count changes when an item is added to the list.

*Your usage of PexObserve is correct. It aids in debugging by providing useful information during the testing process. 

*If you find the contracts inadequate, you need to investigate where they break. To do this, you should look at the results in PexExplorer. In the Explorer, under your test run, you can drill through from the list of failing inputs to the details of the failing inputs. This will help you identify which condition breaks your contract.


Remember to make sure your tests adequately cover different cases in your code and that they verify the correct behavior of your code where contracts are involved. You should generally be testing all possible ways your code paths could be executed.

