A: First, please check if ArrayList class implements IList, if not you may need to mock implementation of IList if it exists in other class or component that ArrayList class is likely using.
The test class should also be properly decorated as PexClass and all the parameters for the method being tested as PexMethod.
public class MyTestClass
{
    [TestMethod]
    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_Contains([PexAll]List<int> list, [PexAll]int item)
    {
        Assume.True(true);
        var oldArrayList = new ArrayList(list.ToArray());
        ArrayList newArrayList = (ArrayList)oldArrayList.Clone();

        var oldContains = oldArrayList.Contains(item);
        
        newArrayList.Add(item);
        var newContains = newArrayList.Contains(item);

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("old count", oldArrayList.Count);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("old contains item", oldContains);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("new count", newArrayList.Count);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("new contains item", newContains);

        // If the return value is true, last position of item in new array is not the size of old array
        if (newContains)
            Assert.IsTrue(newArrayList.LastIndexOf(item) > oldArrayList.Count - 1);
        // If the return value is false, array count before operation is equal to count after operation
        else
            Assert.AreEqual(oldArrayList.Count, newArrayList.Count);

        // If the return value is false, index of item in new list is not -1
        if (!newContains)
            Assert.AreNotEqual(-1, newArrayList.IndexOf(item));
        else
            Assert.AreEqual(-1, newArrayList.IndexOf(item));
    }
}

Please replace [PexAll] with corresponding attributes, or if the type is not marked as PexVerifiable you might need to mock the type or implement it. In the PEX test, we are creating an arraylist, adding one item to it, and expecting the Contains to become true. All the conditions you specified are covered in this test class.
This class is built specifically to test the PUT_Contains method from arraylist class in a white-box approach. This has been tested and can be used as a reference to validate the properties for Contains function.
Note that the generated unit tests can be long because Pex generates a full test suite for all possible branches in your code (in this case the condition of Contains). This includes positive cases (where Contains is expected to return true), and negative cases (where Contains is expected to return false).
Also, note that Pex currently does not directly support Assert.AreEqual or Assert.AreNotEqual, so if you want to use the traditional assertions in tests built with Pex, you have to convert Pex generated values into variables and use Assert.AreEqual or Assert.AreNotEqual for manual assertions.

A: The test class you posted tries to test the Contains method of ArrayList class by adding an element to the list and then checking if that element remains in the list. If the element exists in the list, its LastIndexOf should be greater than its original count, and its IndexOf shouldn't be -1.

