A: You should indeed focus on getting your tests in C#. However, the Pex Testing tool you are using, and by extension your question, seems to be directed towards creating test cases with C# and Visual Studio.

Since the VB.Net project you have is in VB.Net, you will need to make sure the Pex tests are written in VB.Net. A quick Google search shows most Pex users are using C#, so you might want to consider finding an alternative that supports VB.Net.

Another possibility is converting your tests into VB.Net. You would need to understand some VB.Net syntax, such as method/function definitions, and understanding what the various statements mean in this language.

Please note that since you have a mixed language environment, testing should be separate for each language. You may want to consider testing each language separately as it will certainly add complexity as you have to maintain separate test classes for different languages.

Since Pex tests can be quite broad and would cover almost all of the code, just make sure to have an assertion that checks your conditions for Contains method. It's important to note that Pex won't execute if the condition is not met and it won't create an invalid test method either.

But in short, you should test your application using the programming language you are using in VB.Net project and sticking to that same tool if possible. VB.Net syntax can get complicated, so it can be a good learning experience working with a language that has a unique syntax.

Hope this helps.

A: Converting Pex tests is a rather complicated task. Pex is a static analysis tool that injects tests into your code, so you will not directly be able to convert them.

If the objective is to test your vb.net code using the Pex tool, you should write your tests in C#. They cannot be reused directly in your VB.Net project because the Pex tool uses C# syntax.

You should have your test methods written in VB.net since you have your code written in VB.net. You should consider changing your test cases from C# to VB.Net because the VB.net project you have is being coded in VB.net.

Here you will have the VB.NET syntax in your tests:

1. Change the testing attributes to attributes used in Visual Basic.NET.
2. Change the method signatures to VB.NET syntax.
3. Change the 'Assert' syntax to VB.NET syntax.
4. In the test method itself, construct test logic using VB.NET syntax.

It's a complex task given the amount of VB.NET syntax being utilized in Pex tests. If you do not know how to write Pex tests in VB.NET, I would recommend learning VB.NET and Pex in C# first. Then, you could write your Pex tests in C# and then transfer them to VB.NET.

There is no direct way to convert Pex tests between C# and VB.NET.

